{"title":"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 22","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Trig)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Trig)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Interactive_Teaching_Approach)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Interactive Teaching Approach)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_&amp;_More_by_MegaMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution &amp; More by MegaMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Claire_Wang_with_explanation_of_concept\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Claire Wang with explanation of concept</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are chosen independently and uniformly at random from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e861e10e1c19918756b9c8b7717684593c63aeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />. Which of the following numbers is closest to the probability that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/2/692e3bee67c91837acbdcf63862b3b3c94366f28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are the side lengths of an obtuse triangle?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/6/8e6b821ccfacbf03c7c69737b5e09b697fa580d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)} \\text{ 0.21} \\qquad \\textbf{(B)} \\text{ 0.25} \\qquad \\textbf{(C)} \\text{ 0.29} \\qquad \\textbf{(D)} \\text{ 0.50} \\qquad \\textbf{(E)} \\text{ 0.79}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"451\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The Pythagorean Inequality tells us that in an obtuse triangle, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/5/3c5379f5d32c8aa53b931a3d02777cb823667588.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{2} + b^{2} &lt; c^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"16\" />. The triangle inequality tells us that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/5/6e53875273f32921a1a89ab9db9712dce14a12c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a + b &gt; c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"72\" height=\"14\" />. So, we have two inequalities:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/685ce0f4c81b985ebdf3b12a304a7164f6ef7fdb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2 + y^2 &lt; 1\\]\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/1/9b146881400080038376bdbc10aaa2b1a4d5c7e3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x + y &gt; 1\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" />\nThe first equation is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/d/4cd0f9f9869b39e79527f9010293adf7bfe5210f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of a circle with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the second equation is a line from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/5/6457ca2dcd40dc21d9aa0db18c66bbac9af4d899.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/754e946d1a160f767f0f61cbd9460401c2d1c17f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />.\nSo, the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/4/2b42260b5f06579931763029155165d6e19c6abe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"37\" /> which is approximately <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/d/f2db617d52ee41152fe4de6819b8a141d1b51d34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)} ~0.29}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Trig.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Trig)\">Solution 2 (Trig)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Trig)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that the obtuse angle in the triangle has to be opposite the side that is always length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This is because the largest angle is always opposite the largest side, and if two sides of the triangle were <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the last side would have to be greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to make an obtuse triangle. Using this observation, we can set up a law of cosines where the angle is opposite <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/9/1e95ff285cfb994994570e80bb780ba0ab36f362.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1^2=x^2+y^2-2xy\\cos(\\theta)\\]\" width=\"196\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are the sides that go from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e861e10e1c19918756b9c8b7717684593c63aeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the angle opposite the side of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>By isolating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/7/42781e6e22b30f45c904fcccdaffa1124d6a2d77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos(\\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"18\" />, we get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/6/ee6edb6d1ef156550385739f8a36b14c36769c78.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1-x^2-y^2}{-2xy} = \\cos(\\theta)\\]\" width=\"160\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to be obtuse, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/7/42781e6e22b30f45c904fcccdaffa1124d6a2d77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos(\\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"18\" /> must be negative. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/0/450aa973504ae1001f5c16033d19df648256e8d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1-x^2-y^2}{-2xy}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"90\" height=\"42\" /> is negative. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> must be positive, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/7/d37a99eae395e6d88105348b2631a753b7e5a511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> must be negative, so we must make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/b/1cbf28e529622c424171678d8c514bf2c8d21762.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-x^2-y^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> positive. From here, we can set up the inequality\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1fa75ff984a201f91b288e43f2464bedc5b4cc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2+y^2&lt;1\\]\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" />\nAdditionally, to satisfy the definition of a triangle, we need:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/c/04c70b2605e904125f8375833511a94f06645ac1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x+y&gt;1\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" />\nThe solution should be the overlap between the two equations in the first quadrant.\n</p><p>By observing that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/f/b3f94fb2775e1479bea1b05f83d7ac56ab282c60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> is the equation for a circle, the amount that is in the first quadrant is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/d/11d3791d908578e9ede8da9a29fefa2429468419.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"13\" height=\"33\" />. The line can also be seen as a chord that goes from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/5/6457ca2dcd40dc21d9aa0db18c66bbac9af4d899.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/754e946d1a160f767f0f61cbd9460401c2d1c17f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. By cutting off the triangle of area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> that is not part of the overlap, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/5/e3522d08caa5aef00549db3d1f3d3bd98f28a5a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} \\approx \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)} ~0.29}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"154\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>..why would you do this? for what purpose? its much more complicated and this is the AMC 10! -Orion 2010\n</p><p>Edit: This is just a way to prove the pythagorean inequality if you don't already know it.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Interactive_Teaching_Approach.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Interactive_Teaching_Approach)\">Video Solution (Interactive Teaching Approach)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Interactive Teaching Approach)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OLIw8P8Aas4\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OLIw8P8Aas4</a>\n</p><p>~MathkidsbyMax\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.26_More_by_MegaMath\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_&amp;_More_by_MegaMath\">Video Solution &amp; More by MegaMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution &amp; More by MegaMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d6oFfN5N_70\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d6oFfN5N_70</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/LwtoLiBwO-E?t=316\">https://youtu.be/LwtoLiBwO-E?t=316</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/tWkE_c3Fa3I\">https://youtu.be/tWkE_c3Fa3I</a> -- Geometric Probability and Inequalities!\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GHAMU60rI5c\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?